    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d330">

                <pb facs="0278" n="266" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg266"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00111A.R./937: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-01-17T14:00:00-03:00">January 17, 1940—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 3 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>25. The meeting yesterday afternoon of the Neutrality Committee was an
                    organizational one.<anchor n="22" xml:id="d330-f1"/> The question was
                    immediately raised as to whether the Committee is to sit in permanent session
                    for the duration of the war or whether it is to work rapidly for a few weeks and
                        <pb facs="0279" n="267" xml:id="pg267"/> then adjourn for a month or two and
                    meet again and sit for a longer period. Our representative and the
                    representatives of Mexico and Costa Rica are in favor of the second
                    alternative.</p>
                <p>Aranha tells me that the only matter that the Brazilian Government will suggest
                    at this juncture for the consideration of the Committee is the matter of
                    belligerent interference with neutral mails.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d330-f1" n="22" xml:id="d330-f1-content">
                <p>The members of the Neutrality Committee at that time were: Argentina, Luis
                    Podestá Costa; Brazil, Afranio de Mello Franco; Chile, Mariano Fontecilla; Costa
                    Rica, Alejandro Aguilar Machado; Mexico, Roberto Córdova; United States, Charles
                    G. Fenwick; Venezuela, Gustavo Herrera.</p><p>See <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Minutes of the Sessions Held by the Inter-American Neutrality
                        Committee</hi>, Appendix A, Law and Treaty Series No. 15.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
